    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d431">

                <pb facs="0518" n="420" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg420"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/693</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs at Nanking to
                        the Chinese Legation</hi><anchor n="65" xml:id="d431-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-12-14">December 14, 1932</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The news of the resumption of diplomatic relations with Soviet Russia was
                    favorably commented in the Chinese press. There was a nation-wide expression of
                    satisfaction and the government was praised.</p>
                <p>Commenting on the United States government’s unwillingness to participate in the
                    conciliation committee, <hi rendition="#italic">Chiu Kuo Jih Pao</hi> says in
                    his editorial today that the reason seems to lie in the contrary views
                    maintained by Sir John Simon and Secretary Stimson regarding the Manchurian
                    question. It would appear that Simon is inclined to acquiesce in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">fait accompli</hi> whereas Stimson stands for the
                    non-recognition of any status created by the force of arms.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
